<!--Post: 3709--><!--Time: 1105148917-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3709"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 5:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Has anyone here explored the idea that BTK went to Wichita High School Southeast? Lee Streiff certainly would have taught &#34;o Death&#34; type folklore in his Engish and American literature classes there in the late sixties - which would fit the profile timeline. Also, the same area of town where the Otero&#39;s lived I think? If he did live with his mother - and maybe inherited her house in that area - he may still be there. Can any check an old yearbook?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3709-->
<!--Post: 3714--><!--Time: 1105151323-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3714"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 6:28 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Who would they check for?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3714-->
<!--Post: 3719--><!--Time: 1105159472-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3719"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 8:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Since WPD is so hot on the &#34;O Death&#34; thing, I think this is an interesting alternative angle to what they&#39;re currently pursuing.  There is probably as much reaon to speculate that BTK passed through Southeast High English as there is to think that he passed through WSU English - possibly more, given the required nature of the former if he lived near there.  The bigger question is whether this or another teacher actually DID teach that song, or use a book that included it.  If a definite connection can be made, it could be a very helpful, IMO.  Along those lines, I would personally encourage anybody with information on a new &#34;O Death&#34; connection to give that info to the WPD, respond here, or both. <BR> <BR>Lilymarie - can you give me a rough square of the roadways that border the area that feeds this high school, particularly in the &#39;60&#39;s in case it changed?  Also, roughly what years this teacher taught there, to the best of your knowledge.  I&#39;m curious.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3719-->
<!--Post: 3722--><!--Time: 1105160226-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3722"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wade<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wade<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 8:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Someone who had enough time could come up with a short list to look at there.  Take out the black population since I think it is fairly ruled out in most case profiles I have seen.  Being at SE you could rule out quite a few that way.  Then elimnate people who are dead now.  Again, that would wipe out quite a few.  Then you check on males who moved after high school and made a life somewhere else.  <BR>&#40;THIS WILL TAKE YOU FOREVER...HOPEFULLY YOU HAVE NO JOB TO KEEP&#41; <BR>However it could done.  Use a couple of things we ALL assume.  For 1 he had a WSU connection, so maybe further prune the list down again, etc, etc. etc. <BR> <BR>With the population of the school and the number of people you COULD rule out fairly quickly using the above information &#40;of which their are things&#41;, one could come up with a list fairly small in size and definitely one that could be pared further with the facts of the case, alibis and etc. <BR>IMO.....you could come up with a list of around 30-50 guys.  Then just look at names and try to make connections.  I&#39;m sure thats what law enfor. is doing with most clues. <BR> <BR>HOWEVER I WOULD NOT START THERE.  JUST SHOWING YOU IT IS POSSIBLE TO OBTAIN A SMALL LIST WITH A LITTLE WORK.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3722-->
<!--Post: 3739--><!--Time: 1105163936-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3739"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 9:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thank you Wade and Pax. That&#39;s exactly where I was going with this line of thought. I am hoping someone with more history at WHS East or Southeast could do a bit of research. 1957 was WHS Southeast first year - and some of the other schools were fed into Southeast including Planeview and East. WHS SE is very, very close&#40;near Oliver and Douglas&#41; to the Otero and other BTK known sites. If you search Lee Streiff who taught American/English Literature &amp; Folklore at Southeast - you will see that he is connected very tightly with many famous &#34;beatnik&#34; folklore writers of that generation including Ginsberg, Kourac, Michael McClure &#40;Streiff and McClure were HS buddies&#41; - famous for &#34;The Beard&#34; about Billy the Kid &#40;BTK&#41;. And something weird I just found out - an actor named &#34;Richard Bright&#34; played BTK - Billy The Kid in McClure&#39;s production in San Francisco version of The Beard.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3739-->
